WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.197 --> 00:00:01.530
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Hi, I'm Alicia.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.060
And today, I'll
be talking to you

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.910
about how to apply best
practices of REST APIs

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.488
to GraphQL.

00:00:07.488 --> 00:00:11.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.142 --> 00:00:19.120
GraphQL is an open source
data query and manipulation

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.360
language for APIs and a
runtime for fulfilling queries

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.070
with existing data.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.980
It's a new way to
think about APIs.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.080
With GraphQL, you
can send queries

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.250
to get exactly the data you're
looking for in one request.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.890
GraphQL can enable
front and backend teams

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.100
to collaborate more smoothly
when developing applications.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.180
Let's look at an example
of a GraphQL query.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.470
Here, we are querying for user
data with the ID of the user.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.170
The first thing to note about
the query and the response

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.010
is that they are identical.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.010
This is because
GraphQL is typed,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.880
meaning that what you ask
for is what you get back.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.920
We are simply just saying,
get me the user with this ID

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.760
and return me back their
name, posts, and followers.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.940
Now let's compare this to how
we would get the same results

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.950
using a REST endpoint.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.800
Here, we have had to define
three separate endpoints

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.430
in order to have the ability
to access the same data.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.930
Some of you might
say that you could

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.850
reduce this down to
just one endpoint

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.660
by returning the entire user
object in the first request.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.400
This would be seen as
overfetching of data.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.420
One key difference to note
here is that with GraphQL, you

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.040
know which objects
and data you have

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.590
available with each
query, so you can specify

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.560
the shape of the data you would
like back from the server.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.720
With REST, you
can't really define

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.320
what is returned to you,
as the server decides this.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.810
So we can think of GraphQL
as a single endpoint

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.090
upon which we have a set
of executable queries

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.680
that map to a
schema of the data.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.330
There are three key benefits
of using this approach.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.280
First of all, all
data exchange is

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.470
performed at a single endpoint.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.020
That means granularity
for data load,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.170
execution of requests in
parallel, and query planning.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.430
It also means if one resolver
fails, the rest of the query

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.470
can still resolve and
return useful data.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.670
Secondly, the real strength
is in the query language.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.430
You can declare which schemas
you want the data from

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.640
and the format of that data,
which prevents overfetching

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.500
and underfetching of data.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.410
And thirdly, availability
of native integration

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.620
with the ReactJS UI framework
and other client libraries

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.850
considerably increases
productivity for developers.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.347
GraphQL is great, but how
does it compare to REST?

00:02:34.347 --> 00:02:36.430
There are a number of
differences between the two,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.130
and I'll highlight
four of these.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.020
When we compare
the endpoints, REST

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.870
makes many smaller
requests to many endpoints

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.520
and the URL taxonomy is used as
a logical API resource manager.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.730
GraphQL, on the other
hand, makes requests

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.980
to one endpoint, which results
in longer and larger queries

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.080
for customer data.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.980
REST uses HTTP verbs and
generally uses JSON XML

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.260
in order to exchange that data.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.580
GraphQL uses POST
most of the time

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.940
and also leverages
different query types

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.810
specified inside the
protocol, not in HTTP.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.030
Even though GraphQL
uses a custom query

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.630
language on the request,
JSON is returned as a result.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.480
When we compare
documentation, we'll

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.090
see that REST uses open
API specifications,

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.500
while GraphQL uses a
schema-generated documentation.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.830
Frequently, we see customers
using GraphiQL or GraphQL

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:34.720
Playground to interact with
schema-based GraphQL endpoints.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.000
From a discovery
perspective, the portal

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.100
offers a veritable
playground for developers

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.280
to try out APIs before they
use them in their code,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.800
while GraphQL comes with
a built-in playground that

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.100
enables users to explore new
queries on the fly, including

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.650
automatic tab completion.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.600
While the two solutions
are very different,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.420
there are a number of best
practices in REST APIs that

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.710
can be applied to GraphQL.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.940
Let's quickly look at
these best practices.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.480
Consistency in GraphQL starts
with well-formed GraphQL.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.450
And we can apply the best
practices of well-formed REST

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.130
here.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.250
Here's an example.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.340
You would never expect
a well-formed REST query

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.770
to be a list of
employees by department.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.820
Rather, a well-formed REST
query would be a resource.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.950
A graph hierarchy
is fundamentally

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.780
a data-driven hierarchy.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.700
It is not a
functional hierarchy,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.380
like an object model.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.900
We should be treating
GraphQL like Java functions.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.480
In other words, be
data-oriented, not

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.670
function-oriented.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.230
The second best
practice is around

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.270
submitting and retrieving data.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.060
Those are not always
mirrored operations.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.820
In REST, we frequently
submit and receive data

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.350
from different URLs.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.120
In particular, when
retrieving data

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.030
from multiple services in a
microservice architecture,

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.340
Command Query
Responsibility Segregation--

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.710
CQRS-- is common.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.380
As we think about how
this applies to GraphQL,

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.600
we need to consider that query
mutations can get very complex

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.620
very quickly, especially with
many different types when

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.540
submitting very little data.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:09.970
Applying CQRS to GraphQL can
help us avoid this challenge.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.910
The third is optimizing
for reusability.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:16.210
In REST, we think a lot
about our URL taxonomy,

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.690
pagination, and filtering,
because developers can then

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:23.470
predict what the behavior
will be of any API they call.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:27.190
In GraphQL, we should
be doing the same thing.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.680
With GraphQL, it is important
to be careful with schema

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.810
stitching, because
to the developer,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.480
the representation looks
like one big graph.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.080
But the behavior of the JSON
returned might be different.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.710
So relay cursor
connections and input hints

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.570
should all present
uniform behavior,

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:47.860
regardless of the portion of
the graph being requested.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.970
Even with the best
practices I mentioned,

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.870
there are a number of challenges
with GraphQL deployments.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.730
These revolve around
authentication

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:56.290
and authorization.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.350
For example, GraphQL
doesn't solve authorization

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.000
and does not support
authorization

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.290
for schema browsing.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:04.120
GraphQL doesn't
support a standard

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.010
for throttling or quotas.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.410
It does not have a
unified view of analytics,

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:11.380
which means expanding the view
of APIs from simple metrics

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.960
to business and
operational impact metrics

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.460
can be challenging.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.470
And it is based on a
single endpoint, which

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.110
means that there are no
URL versions and no support

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.060
for header or format versioning.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.280
An API management
solution can help

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.220
with managing the
challenges of GraphQL APIs.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.380
An API management
platform gives you

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.560
control over and visibility
into the APIs that connect

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.310
application and data
cross-cloud or on-premises.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:39.730
Managing your GraphQL APIs
with an API management platform

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.130
enables you to easily
manage authentication

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.580
and authorization, implement
throttling or quotas,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.570
view detailed analytics,
and streamline versioning.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.240
To learn more and get
started, check out the links

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:53.800
in the description below.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

